+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * Dynamic lower layer support for overlayfs.
+ *
+ * Allows adding a read-only lower layer to a mounted overlay
+ * (remount with "lowerdir+=<path>") and removing an idle one
+ * (remount with "lowerdir-=<path>").  Designed for live distro
+ * module activation (PorteuX/Porteus/Slax style), where squashfs
+ * images are activated/deactivated on the running root filesystem.
+ *
+ * Rules and design:
+ *
+ *  - Only enabled when the overlay was mounted with "maxlayers=<n>";
+ *    the layer and fs arrays are allocated with that capacity up front
+ *    so that ovl_layer/ovl_sb addresses stay stable for the lifetime
+ *    of the mount (ovl_entry stacks reference them by pointer).
+ *
+ *  - A newly added layer is inserted at the TOP of the read-only
+ *    stack (directly below the upper layer), i.e. it overrides all
+ *    older lower layers, like aufs "add:1".
+ *
+ *  - Priority is defined by the order of the root dentry's ovl_entry
+ *    stack (which every lookup derives from), NOT by the index in
+ *    ofs->layers[].  layer->idx == array slot, stable for the layer's
+ *    lifetime.
+ *
+ *  - Layer ADDITION updates the cached state surgically: the walk in
+ *    ovl_dyn_surgical_add() touches only what the module provides,
+ *    keeping the dentry cache warm.  If that is not possible,
+ *    staleness is handled via a generation stamp in the high bits of
+ *    d_fsdata (see ovl_dentry_gen()), inherited from the parent at
+ *    instantiation time: bumping the generation makes every dentry
+ *    (including negatives) fail d_revalidate on its next use and be
+ *    looked up again through the fresh root.
+ *
+ *  - Layer REMOVAL never bumps the generation: lookups are locked out
+ *    for its whole duration, the dcache shrink plus inode eviction
+ *    flush everything unpinned (including negative dentries that the
+ *    removed layer's whiteouts created), and the surviving in-use
+ *    directories are rebuilt in place - the surviving cache is
+ *    consistent as-is.  A bump would only leave pinned dentries
+ *    (process CWDs, fd-held dirs, which no fresh lookup can replace)
+ *    permanently stale, breaking fd-based path re-resolution
+ *    (/proc/self/fd, as used by FUSE and firejail) with ESTALE.
+ *
+ *  - Removal only succeeds if no inode (other than the root) still
+ *    references the layer, after dropping all unpinned cache entries.
+ *    Otherwise -EBUSY is returned, matching aufs/Slax semantics
+ *    ("kill the processes using files from the module first").
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 PorteuX
+ */
+
